# Reverse a string over {A,B,C,D}. Markov's own algorithm, not a new one.
#
#   rw progs/m10.rw 'ABCD'   ->  DCBA
#
# This is Algorithm M10 from Nick Cercone's CSE6390 notes on Markov (normal)
# algorithms, transcribed rule for rule. The notes write it as five production
# schemas over an unspecified alphabet:
#
#   M101: [conclusive]  §b  -> .        (b is the marker beta)
#   M102:               §db -> bd
#   M103:               §df -> f§d
#   M104:               §d  -> bd
#   M105:               W   -> §        (W is the empty word)
#
# where d and f range over the alphabet. rw has no variables, so each schema
# is written out once per symbol -- M103 costs |A|^2 rules, which is why the
# alphabet is four letters and not twenty-six. § is $ here and beta is @.
#
# M105 is Markov's empty word: it matches before the first symbol, so it
# prepends, and it always applies. That is why it is last -- it is the rule
# that runs when nothing else can, and the conclusive M101 is what stops the
# algorithm rather than running out of applicable rules.
#
# The method: $ walks rightwards through the untouched suffix (M103) carrying
# one symbol with it, drops it at the boundary @ (M102) or makes a boundary if
# there is not one yet (M104), and M105 starts the next pass. The notes trace
# ABCD to DCBA in sixteen steps; test.sh asserts that trace.
$@->.
$A@->@A
$B@->@B
$C@->@C
$D@->@D
$AA->A$A
$AB->B$A
$AC->C$A
$AD->D$A
$BA->A$B
$BB->B$B
$BC->C$B
$BD->D$B
$CA->A$C
$CB->B$C
$CC->C$C
$CD->D$C
$DA->A$D
$DB->B$D
$DC->C$D
$DD->D$D
$A->@A
$B->@B
$C->@C
$D->@D
->$
